WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.133
Your pod died overnight.

2
00:00:02.153 --> 00:00:04.755
Nobody noticed until a customer did.

3
00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:07.255
A bare pod is a single point of failure.

4
00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:09.590
You need something that never sleeps.

5
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.890
This port has three controllers: the Deployment, the Job, and the DaemonSet.

6
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.425
First: the controller you use most.

7
00:00:17.445 --> 00:00:18.875
You say: I want three.

8
00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:21.335
A Deployment keeps three pods running.

9
00:00:21.355 --> 00:00:23.248
Delete a pod, or lose a node.

10
00:00:23.268 --> 00:00:25.985
A new pod appears in seconds.

11
00:00:26.005 --> 00:00:28.945
Second: ship a new version with no downtime.

12
00:00:28.965 --> 00:00:31.145
New pods start, one by one.

13
00:00:31.165 --> 00:00:34.380
An old pod leaves only when a new pod is ready.

14
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.517
A bad release?

15
00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:39.005
One command takes you back to the last version.

16
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:41.115
Third: some work has an end.

17
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:43.503
A Job runs a pod until it succeeds.

18
00:00:43.523 --> 00:00:46.300
A CronJob starts a Job on a schedule.

19
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.975
A DaemonSet runs one copy on every node.

20
00:00:48.995 --> 00:00:51.525
Use it for log agents.

21
00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:53.610
Quick recap of workloads.

22
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.122
Deployments heal and roll.

23
00:00:55.142 --> 00:00:56.197
Jobs finish.

24
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:58.570
DaemonSets cover every node.

25
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.690
Now start the first lesson below.
